====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. Class size distribution (7 classes):  { 5, 3, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1 }
   The long tail of singletons is nearly half the classes.

2. Biggest class (5) is the predicted 1-hop collision.
   Chain-middle edges `R(c2,c3)`, `R(c3,c4)` merge with all three
   cycle edges into one class. From this class's perspective, a
   chain and a cycle are indistinguishable. The largest "pattern
   candidate" on raw class size is a known false-merge.

3. Genuine cross-structure merge at size 2: chain tail + tree leaf.
   `R(c4,c5)` and `R(t2,t4)` share the compound class
   ((Both-1-1, RightOnly-0-1), (0,0,0,1)).
   Structurally: "an edge whose left endpoint has one predecessor
   and right endpoint is terminal." This IS a real structural
   similarity — both are "the edge entering a leaf from a
   non-leaf." Calling this a pattern would capture the abstraction
   "terminal descent," which seems legitimate.

4. Star spokes form a clean within-component class (size 3).
   Three edges, all in the star, all with identical compound
   signatures. This is the most straightforward pattern candidate:
   clear repetition, confined to one component, no confusion with
   other structures.

5. Tree's three edges split into three classes.
   Despite being visually a small tree, the tree's edges end up in
   three different singleton classes (R(t1,t2), R(t1,t3), R(t2,t4)).
   The first two share identifier x = t1 but differ in y's profile;
   the third has reverse neighborhood. The 1-hop compound signature
   is fine-grained enough to split a small tree into each edge
   being a "kind of its own." A pattern-mining layer that requires
   class size > 1 would find no pattern in this tree.

6. Chain head is a singleton.
   `R(c1,c2)` is alone in its class — no other edge has the
   "LeftOnly source + Both-middle target + forward=1 only" profile.
   This is correct (it is genuinely unique in the graph) but it
   means "chain head" is not a reusable pattern candidate unless
   we see more chains or relax the criterion.

====================================================================
READING THIS AGAINST (ii) — WHAT COUNTS AS A NAMEABLE PATTERN
====================================================================

The probe was set up to help β answer: "what counts as a nameable
pattern?" The results suggest a concrete answer with caveats:

A minimum-viable criterion for the first β mechanism:
  - A compound class with size ≥ 2 is a *candidate* pattern.
  - Candidate is promoted to *named pattern* only if a sanity-check
    passes.

The probe shows that size alone is insufficient because:
  - The biggest candidate (size 5) is a known 1-hop false merge.
  - Several real patterns (star, "terminal descent") are size 2–3.

The sanity check needs to filter the known collisions. Two forms
available without new machinery:

  (a) **2-hop tie-breaker.** For every class with size > 1, verify
      that the class's members agree at 2-hop neighborhood
      (forward-of-forward, reverse-of-reverse, etc.) before naming.
      This resolves chain-vs-cycle: chain middles have no 2-hop
      closure back; cycle edges do.

  (b) **Subgraph coherence.** Rather than treating edges in a class
      as independent, check whether their *connected span* is itself
      a named candidate pattern. The 3 star spokes all share the
      identifier `s`; the chain middles share a connected path; the
      cycle edges share a closed loop. These are different graph-
      level facts even when the edges individually have the same
      fingerprint.

Of the two, (b) is more aligned with the design-notes goal: naming
patterns like "integer chain" requires subgraph-level thinking.
(a) is a cheaper fix but addresses only the specific collision.

The probe also surfaces a secondary insight: some patterns are
"cross-structure" (chain tail + tree leaf), which means pattern
naming is not always component-local. β should handle this case
cleanly — possibly by keeping the compound class flat across
components and only splitting into per-component subgraph instances
when (b)'s subgraph-coherence check runs.

====================================================================
READING AGAINST (iii) — GAMMA STATUS
====================================================================

This probe is still deterministic derivation: given an RSet, the
fingerprint and class assignment are fully determined. Drive (γ)
is still dormant.

But we now see the *first candidate* decision that drive will have
to make: "of these 7 classes, which should become named patterns?"
Naive policies ("name every size ≥ 2 class") would name the wrong
things first (the 5-member false-merge). So γ can't be trivial.

γ's first real job appears to be: apply some quality filter on
candidate classes — minimally (a) or (b) above — and name only
those that pass. γ therefore needs the filter to exist first, which
means it must be scoped alongside β or directly after.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0007 probe is complete. Its findings give a concrete starting
shape for β:

  β layer = {
      - candidate generation: compound class with size ≥ 2
      - sanity filter: either 2-hop tie-breaker (cheap) or subgraph
                       coherence check (closer to design-notes goal)
      - naming: compound classes that pass the filter become named
                patterns, encoded as meta-R instances (commitment 3)
  }

The three β sub-questions from earlier:
  (i)  encoding shape — still open; depends on whether we pick
       flat-class naming or subgraph naming.
  (ii) what counts — answered: pass the candidate + filter pipeline
       above, specifics in (a) vs (b).
  (iii) drive — has first concrete role: run the filter on candidates
       and choose what gets named.

Recommendation for next step:

  Do NOT jump to a full β yet. First decide between (a) and (b) —
  they point to very different β shapes. A focused follow-up probe
  or design discussion is warranted. Options for how to proceed:

  Option 1: "Flat-class β." Implement (a), name compound classes
    directly as meta-R instances. Simpler; patterns are edge-kinds.
    Closer to the current code's grain.

  Option 2: "Subgraph β." Implement (b), name connected subgraph
    instances. Closer to design-notes' integer-chain target.
    Requires new machinery (connected-span detection, subgraph
    canonicalization).

  Option 3: "Staged β." Option 1 first (names edge-kinds), then
    Option 2 on top (names subgraph kinds). Incremental, but spends
    effort on Option 1 that might not be reused by Option 2.

Leaning toward Option 2: it directly addresses the design-notes
goal and avoids committing effort to edge-kind naming that
subgraph-kind naming would subsume. But this is a scope choice
the user should make before β starts.
